s with non-sunny lines (vertical/horizontal/diagonal) and at most 2 sunny lines.\n\nObservation: Any sunny line can intersect a column at at most one point (since a non-vertical line can intersect a vertical line at at most one point). Actually a sunny line can intersect a given vertical line x = i at at most one point (unless it's vertical, which it's not). So each sunny line can cover at most one point in each column. So to cover column n-2 with three points, we need at least three lines intersecting that column. If we have at most 2 sunny lines, we need at least one non-sunny line that covers at least two points in that column. The only non-sunny lines that can intersect a column at more than one point are vertical lines (which intersect at all points in that column) and diagonal lines (x+y=c) which intersect column i at at most one point (since x+y=c => y=c-i, unique y). Horizontal lines intersect column i at at most one point (since y=c). So only vertical lines can cover multiple points in a column. So to cover column n-2's three points, we need either a vertical line x=n-2, or we need to use at least three lines (sunny or non-sunny) intersecting that column. Since we have at most 2 sunny lines, we need at least one vertical line x=n-2. So we must have vertical line x=n-2.\n\nNow, consider column n-1 with two points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Since we have vertical line x=n-2, we still have at most 2 sunny lines left. To cover column n-1, we could use a vertical line x=n-1 (non-sunny) covering both points. Or we could use two lines (sunny or non-sunny) each covering one point. But we have only 2 sunny lines total, and we already used one vertical line x=n-2 (non-sunny). So we have n-1 remaining lines: n-2 non-sunny lines (including possibly vertical x=n-1) and up to 2 sunny lines. But we need to cover column n-1. If we don't use vertical line x=n-1, we need to cover its two points with two distinct lines, each intersecting column n-1 at one point. Those could be sunn