e y = constant at most once (since not horizontal). Also, each sunny line can intersect each line x+y=c at most once (since not slope -1). So a sunny line can intersect each of those families at most once.\n\nThus a sunny line can cover at most one point per column, per row, per diagonal (x+y constant). So the maximum number of points a sunny line can cover among the points in the triangle is limited by the number of columns (n) but also by the number of rows (n) and diagonals (n). However, the points are arranged such that each column has decreasing number of points.\n\nWe need to cover all points with n lines. Let's denote the set of points as P = {(a,b) | a,b ≥ 1, a+b ≤ n+1}. The number of points is N = n(n+1)/2.\n\nWe need to partition P into n lines (some may intersect at points). The lines can overlap in covering points; we don't need a partition, just a covering.\n\nGoal: find possible k.\n\nWe can think of the problem as a covering problem: we have n lines, each line can cover some points. We need to cover all N points.\n\nWe can think of the maximum number of points that can be covered by k sunny lines plus (n-k) non-sunny lines.\n\nNon-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. These can cover multiple points in a column (vertical) or row (horizontal) or diagonal (slope -1). So they can be used to cover many points.\n\nWe need to find constraints on k.\n\nOne approach: Use a counting argument: each sunny line can cover at most one point in each column. So across all columns, the total number of points covered by sunny lines is at most k * (max points per column?). Actually each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, but there are n columns. So each sunny line can cover at most n points (one per column). However, the points are not present in all columns: column i has n+1-i points. So the maximum number of points a sunny line can cover is at most n (if it passes through a point in each column). But can a line pass through a point in e